===Etymology===
{{lbor|en|sa|ओम्}} (symbol {{m|sa|ॐ}}). The former (''om'') is used in both Buddhist and Hindu settings, while the latter (''aum'') is usually used only in Jain and Hindu settings.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɒm/|/əʊm/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/oʊm/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-om.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊm|ɒm|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Hinduism|Buddhism}} A sacred, mystical syllable used in prayer and meditation.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1982|author=w:Lawrence Durrell|title=Constance|publisher=Faber & Faber|year_published=2004|series=Avignon Quintet|page=800
|passage=In fact it sounded exactly like the voice of Livia, her vanished sister, and, like her, it was intoning the '''''Aum''''' just as she used once to do at the beginning of her yoga sessions.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Hazel Curry|journal=The Guardian|date=20 Oct 2001
|passage='''Om''' is pronounced "a-a-o-o-u-u-m-m" and is repeated slowly for as long as possible.}}

